    "summary": "Solid early‑70s set with standout Cumberland Blues and Truckin’",
    "blurb": "The Detroit show delivers a crisp soundboard capture that highlights a tight rendition of Cumberland Blues and an adventurous Truckin’. Energy is mixed – strong playing but frequent tuning breaks and limited segues keep the flow from fully clicking. Overall a worthwhile listen for fans of the band’s early‑70s period.",
    "review": "The October 23, 1971 Easttown Theatre gig captures the Grateful Dead in a transitional moment – the classic early‑70s setlist is performed with the era’s bright guitar tone and Keith Godchaux’s nascent keyboard color. Jerry’s lead work on Cumberland Blues shines, and his quirky lyric turn in Tennessee Jed adds a playful touch. The second‑set highlight comes from the Not Fade Away → Goin' Down The Road Feeling Bad → Not Fade Away sequence, where Phil’s dry‑spoken bass lines drive a jam that feels both loose and purposeful. While the band delivers solid playing throughout, long tuning breaks and a lack of seamless segues hint at tension with an impatient audience, tempering the overall momentum. The Miller soundboard recording (gd1971-10-23.sbd.miller.111673.flac16) offers clear early‑70s fidelity, making this show an enjoyable snapshot for completists despite its uneven energy.",
